WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.109
Unconstrained optimization searches without an additional feasible-set restriction

00:00:04.109 --> 00:00:07.355
For this quadratic, the derivative points uphill

00:00:07.355 --> 00:00:15.700
Moving a small distance opposite its sign moves toward the minimum at two, where the derivative is zero.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.315
Gradient descent subtracts a step size times the derivative

00:00:20.315 --> 00:00:25.473
With step size one quarter and starting point zero, the next input is one

00:00:25.473 --> 00:00:31.367
Repeating gives one and a half, then one and three quarters: the error halves each time.

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:37.017
The error formula explains the step-size requirement for this example

00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:43.977
Its magnitude contracts only when the absolute value of one minus twice alpha is below one

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:50.067
Thus alpha between zero and one converges; larger choices can make the iterates diverge.

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:56.625
A zero derivative is a candidate condition, not a universal minimum certificate

00:00:56.625 --> 00:01:03.637
The cubic has derivative zero at the origin but takes smaller values immediately to its left

00:01:03.637 --> 00:01:09.333
Use additional curvature or structural arguments to decide what a stationary point represents.
